('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Evolution is BEST defined as:', 'Instant creation of new species', 'Only large animals getting stronger', 'Gradual change in the inherited traits of a POPULATION over many generations', 'Individual organisms changing during their lifetime', 'C', 'Evolution happens to populations, not single individuals; traits are passed across generations.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Who is the scientist who first proposed natural selection as the main mechanism of evolution?', 'Charles Darwin', 'Gregor Mendel', 'Antoine Lavoisier', 'Albert Einstein', 'A', 'Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859 based on Galápagos Islands observations.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Natural selection is often summarized as:', 'Traits you acquire during life are passed to offspring', 'Survival and reproduction of organisms BEST FITTED to their environment', 'Only the largest animals survive', 'Strongest always kill the weakest', 'B', '“Fitness” = ability to survive AND produce fertile offspring, not just physical strength.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Any inherited trait that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment is called:', 'Adaptation', 'Variation', 'Fossil', 'Isolation', 'A', 'Example: thick fur in polar bears, long neck in giraffes.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'The natural differences in traits between individuals of the same species (e.g., different beak sizes in finches) is called:', 'Selection', 'Heredity', 'Variation', 'Isolation', 'C', 'Variation must already exist in a population for natural selection to act on it.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'For evolution to happen, useful traits must be able to be passed from parents to offspring. This is called:', 'Isolation', 'Variation', 'Adaptation', 'Heredity / Inheritance', 'D', 'Traits only affect evolution if they are genetic and can be inherited.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Preserved remains, traces, or impressions of ancient organisms found in rock layers are called:', 'Endemic species', 'Fossils', 'Adaptations', 'Homologous structures', 'B', 'Examples: bones, shells, footprints, leaf imprints trapped in sedimentary rock.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Body parts of different species that have SIMILAR BONE STRUCTURE but different functions (e.g., human arm, whale flipper, bat wing) are:', 'Vestigial structures', 'Fossils', 'Homologous structures', 'Analogous structures', 'C', 'Same basic plan = inherited from a common ancestor.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'A body part that has lost most or all of its original function through evolution (e.g., human appendix, tailbone) is:', 'Analogous', 'Adaptation', 'Homologous', 'Vestigial structure', 'D', 'It is reduced in size; remains as evidence of an ancestor that used it fully.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'The study of where different species live on Earth and why they are found there is:', 'Genetics', 'Comparative anatomy', 'Paleontology', 'Biogeography', 'D', 'Explains why islands often have unique species found nowhere else.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'The peppered moth in England changed from mostly light to mostly dark during the Industrial Revolution because tree trunks turned black with soot. This is a famous example of:', 'Biogeography', 'Artificial selection', 'Natural selection in action', 'Vestigial formation', 'C', 'Dark moths survived predation by birds better; they reproduced more and became common.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'A river changes course and permanently splits one population of beetles into two groups that never meet again. This is:', 'Natural selection', 'Artificial selection', 'Reproductive isolation', 'Geographic isolation', 'D', 'Physical barrier (river, mountain, ocean) separates populations; first step to forming new species.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Bird wing and insect wing have the SAME FUNCTION (flying) but completely different internal structure. They are:', 'Vestigial structures', 'Homologous structures', 'Fossils', 'Analogous structures', 'D', 'Same job, different origin = evolved independently, NOT from a common flying ancestor.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Why is isolation important in forming NEW species (speciation)?', 'It creates fossils faster', 'It increases mutation rate instantly', 'It stops gene flow between groups, letting them evolve differently over time', 'It makes organisms weaker', 'C', 'Without contact, separated populations accumulate different traits until they can no longer interbreed.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'pre_test', 'Fossils found in DEEPER / older rock layers are generally:', 'Simpler and more different from living species today', 'Only large mammals', 'Identical to modern organisms', 'More complex than modern life', 'A', 'Fossil record clearly shows simple life first, then gradual change to more complex forms over time.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Which is the BEST evolutionary explanation for giraffes'' long necks?', 'Long-necked variants survived better, reproduced more, and passed the trait on', 'A random mutation made all necks long in one generation', 'They stretched to reach high leaves and passed stretched necks to babies', 'All giraffes chose to grow longer necks', 'A', 'Variation existed first; natural selection favored longer necks over many generations.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Farmers choosing only the largest, sweetest mangoes to plant next season is:', 'Geographic isolation', 'Artificial selection', 'Vestigial selection', 'Natural selection', 'B', 'Humans, not nature, select which traits get passed on.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Early embryos of fish, chicken, pig, and human look very similar. This evidence of common ancestry comes from:', 'DNA sequencing only', 'Comparative embryology', 'Fossil record', 'Biogeography', 'B', 'Shared early developmental stages = inherited from a shared vertebrate ancestor.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Two groups of frogs live in the same pond but mate at different months of the year and never interbreed. This is:', 'Geographic isolation', 'Analogous breeding', 'Reproductive isolation', 'Artificial selection', 'C', 'No physical barrier, but differences in behavior/time prevent mating; they are now separate species.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'The Philippine Eagle is found only in the Philippines and nowhere else on Earth. It is called:', 'An endemic species', 'An artificial species', 'A vestigial species', 'A fossil species', 'A', 'Biogeography: islands and isolated countries evolve many unique endemic species.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Which is the CORRECT sequence of events for a new species to form (speciation)?', 'Isolation → New species → Variation → Selection', 'New species → Variation → Isolation → Selection', 'Variation → Geographic Isolation → Natural Selection → Reproductive Isolation → New species', 'Selection → Variation → Isolation → New species', 'C', 'Variation must exist first; separation stops gene flow; selection makes groups different until they can no longer', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Why is natural selection considered the PRIMARY mechanism of evolution, not mutation, genetic drift, or chance alone?', 'It was the first mechanism discovered', 'It is the only mechanism that CONSISTENTLY produces ADAPTATIONS matched to the environment', 'It is the only mechanism that changes traits', 'It works instantly in one generation', 'B', 'Other mechanisms exist, but only natural selection reliably builds useful, environment-matched traits over time.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Modern whales have tiny, unused pelvic (hip) bones buried deep inside their bodies. This vestigial structure is strong evidence that:', 'Whales evolved from fish directly', 'The pelvic bone has always been useless', 'Whales are still evolving legs today', 'Whales evolved from 4-legged land-dwelling mammals that moved back to the sea', 'D', 'Fossil series confirms this: walking whale → semi-aquatic → fully aquatic modern whale.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Someone says: “Evolution is just a theory, so it’s just a guess.” The CORRECT scientific response is:', 'In science, “theory” means a well-tested explanation supported by massive, repeated evidence from many fields', 'Evolution is only a hypothesis', 'Theories become laws once proven; evolution is almost a law', 'True, it has no evidence yet', 'A', 'Scientific theory ≠ casual “guess”; it is the highest, most reliable level of scientific explanation.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Fossils of the same extinct reptile Mesosaurus are found only in eastern South America and western Africa. This, plus matching coastlines, strongly supports:', 'It evolved independently on both continents', 'Continental drift / Pangaea: these continents were once joined together', 'The species swam across the Atlantic Ocean', 'Fossils were carried by humans', 'B', 'Key biogeography evidence: landmasses split and carried their resident species with them.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Which of the following is NOT a required condition for natural selection to occur?', 'Traits acquired during an organism''s life (e.g., bigger muscles from exercise) are passed to offspring', 'Variation exists in the population', 'Traits are heritable', 'More offspring are born than can survive', 'A', 'This was Lamarck''s WRONG idea; only genetic/inherited traits, not acquired ones, are passed on.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Homologous structures (e.g., forelimbs of vertebrates) are strong evidence of common ancestry mainly because:', 'They perform exactly the same function', 'They appear only in fossil species', 'They share the same unique bone arrangement inherited from a shared ancestor, modified for different jobs', 'They look identical externally', 'C', 'Same basic plan, different uses = descent with modification from one ancestor.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'A severe 10-year drought hits an island; all small, soft seeds die, leaving only large, hard seeds. Over generations, what will natural selection do to the island’s finch population?', 'All finches will instantly grow larger beaks in one year', 'Average beak size will become LARGER, thicker, and stronger', 'Average beak size will get smaller and weaker', 'Beak size will not change at all', 'B', 'Birds with larger, stronger beaks survive and reproduce; the trait becomes more common over generations.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'If two separated populations are NOT geographically isolated and individuals keep moving/mating between them:', 'Mutations stop occurring', 'Natural selection cannot happen at all', 'Gene flow continues; they will NOT diverge into separate species', 'They will definitely become separate species', 'C', 'Constant mixing keeps their gene pools similar; isolation is required for populations to split into new species.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108', 1, 'post_test', 'Which combination of evidence gives the STRONGEST , most reliable support for evolution by common descent?', 'Only DNA similarity alone', 'Only embryology alone', 'Fossil record + homologous structures + DNA/protein similarity + biogeography, all agreeing', 'Only fossil record alone', 'C', 'Multiple independent lines of evidence all converging on the same conclusion is what makes evolution one of the', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000201', 1, 'pre_test', 'The maximum number of individuals an ecosystem can support long-term without exhausting its resources is called:', 'Population density', 'Carrying capacity', 'Birth rate', 'Death rate', 'B', 'Option B is the correct answer.', true),
('b0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000201', 1, 'pre_test', 'Any factor that prevents a population from growing larger is called a:', 'Limiting factor', 'Density factor', 'Growth factor', 'Carrying factor', 'A', 'Option A is the correct answer.', true),
